---
// /mls-photo-requirements/mlslistings/ — MLSListings Rule 7.11.2 (AB 723 amendment).
// Source quotes: docs/mls-sources.md § MLSListings (primary-source check 2026-11-08).
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const path = "/mls-photo-requirements/mlslistings/";
const SOURCE = "https://support.mlslistings.com/s/article/AB723---Altered-Images-Overview";
const title = "MLSListings Altered Photo Rule 7.11.2 Explained";
const h1 = "MLSListings Rule 7.11.2: altered photos, remarks disclosure, and no links";
const description =
  "MLSListings Rule 7.11.2: upload the original, label the altered photo, note any enhancement (color too) in the remarks. No links on photos.";

const faq = [
  {
    q: "Do I have to mention color correction on MLSListings?",
    a: "Yes, in the public remarks. Rule 7.11.2 says any enhancements or modifications to images must be disclosed in the public remarks, and gives color enhancement as an example. AB 723 doesn't require a disclosure for color correction, but this MLS rule does.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use a QR code to the originals instead of uploading them?",
    a: "Not inside the MLS. MLSListings says it does not allow links of any kind from the photos in a listing, and the rule requires you to submit the original, unaltered version. Links and QR codes can still be used on print and social media.",
  },
  {
    q: "What label does MLSListings accept?",
    a: "\"altered\", \"digitally altered\", \"AI altered\", or some accurate, substantially similar term, marked in a reasonably conspicuous manner on the altered image.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does the original have to sit next to the altered photo?",
    a: "The rule text MLSListings published doesn't set a position. It requires you to submit the original. Placing it right after the altered photo costs nothing and makes the pair obvious to buyers.",
  },
  {
    q: "Who do I ask about a specific listing?",
    a: "MLSListings Compliance. Its AB 723 article lists compliance@mlslistings.com and 408-874-0200, option 2.",
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: h1,
    description,
    mainEntityOfPage: `https://retouchlint.com${path}`,
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "RetouchLint" },
    publisher: { "@id": "https://retouchlint.com/#organization" },
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];

const related = [
  { url: "/mls-photo-requirements/", anchor: "All California MLS photo rules compared" },
  { url: "/mls-photo-requirements/crmls/", anchor: "CRMLS: edits it bans outright" },
  { url: "/mls-photo-requirements/bay-east/", anchor: "Bay East MLS altered-photo rule" },
  { url: "/blog/original-image-access/", anchor: "Giving buyers access to the original image" },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  h1={h1}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  related={related}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: "Home", url: "/" },
    { name: "MLS photo requirements", url: "/mls-photo-requirements/" },
    { name: "MLSListings", url: path },
  ]}
  schema={schema}
>
  <p>
    MLSListings Rule 7.11.2 requires three things for an edited listing photo: upload the
    original, unaltered version; mark the altered photo "altered", "digitally altered" or
    "AI altered" (or a substantially similar term); and disclose any enhancement or
    modification in the public remarks, including color enhancement. It also bans links of
    any kind from listing photos, so the link or QR code that AB 723 allows elsewhere
    can't stand in for the original here.
  </p>

  <h2>The rule</h2>
  <p>
    MLSListings' Rules Committee and Board amended Rule 7.11.2, Listing Photograph
    Requirements, in support of AB 723. For altered images, the amended text says:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    "Any enhancements or modifications to images must be disclosed in the public remarks,
    e.g. color enhancement or virtual staging. A Participant or Subscriber who submits any
    digitally created or altered image must also (i) submit the original, unaltered version
    of the digitally altered image, and (ii) ensure that the digitally altered image is
    marked in a reasonably conspicuous manner as "altered," "digitally altered," "AI
    altered," or some accurate, substantially similar, term."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Where it differs from AB 723</h2>
  <h3>Routine enhancements still get a remarks line</h3>
  <p>
    AB 723 excludes lighting, white balance, color correction, exposure and similar
    adjustments from its definition of a digitally altered image, so those photos need no
    statutory statement. MLSListings' remarks requirement covers "any enhancements or
    modifications" and names color enhancement as an example. For a listing whose photos
    were only color-corrected, a short line in the public remarks covers it.
  </p>
  <h3>The original goes in the listing, not behind a link</h3>
  <p>
    The statute lets an advertisement point to the original through a link, URL or QR
    code. MLSListings says it "does not allow links of any kind from the photos in a
    listing" and that QR codes and links belong in other media such as print and social.
    In the MLS, upload the original as its own photo.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked examples</h2>
  <h3>Virtually staged great room</h3>
  <p>
    Mark the staged photo "Digitally Altered", upload the empty-room original, and add a
    sentence to the public remarks such as "Photo 4 is virtually staged; the unstaged
    original is photo 5."
  </p>
  <h3>Every photo color-corrected, nothing added or removed</h3>
  <p>
    No AB 723 statement and no "altered" label are needed, because nothing in the frame
    changed. The remarks still need a note that the photos were color-enhanced.
  </p>
  <h3>Staged photo with a QR code to the originals: fails</h3>
  <p>
    The QR code is a link from a listing photo, which MLSListings doesn't allow, and the
    original still hasn't been submitted. Remove the code, upload the original, and use
    the QR code on the flyer instead. The <a href="/tools/disclosure-generator/">disclosure
    generator</a> writes the flyer statement.
  </p>

  <h2>Other photo requirements in Rule 7.11.2</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A property photo within one day after entry, except Members Only–Do Not Show listings. Existing buildings need a photo of the front.</li>
    <li>No text-only images. Text or overlays are allowed only to describe property characteristics, such as boundaries, pointers or physical descriptions.</li>
    <li>No photo by seller choice: use the MLS-provided "Seller Prefers No Photo" image, and keep the seller's written authorization to provide on request.</li>
    <li>New construction: a realistic rendering of the front until the building is finished, then a photograph.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  {faq.map((f) => (
    <>
      <h3>{f.q}</h3>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </>
  ))}
  <p class="text-sm">
    Source: <a href={SOURCE}>MLSListings, "AB723 – Altered Images Overview"</a>, quoting
    amended Rule 7.11.2. Check the current rule before relying on this summary; this is not
    legal advice.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
